---
<BaseLayout title={title} description={description} image={image} schemaBlocks={[articleSchema]}>
<header class="border-b border-neutral-200 bg-[#f7f2e8] py-10 md:py-16"><div class="container max-w-wide grid items-center gap-8 lg:grid-cols-2"><div><p class="eyebrow">Carton quality · <time datetime="2026-11-28">November 28, 2026</time></p><h1 class="mt-4 font-display text-4xl font-extrabold leading-tight md:text-5xl">{title}</h1><p class="mt-5 text-lg leading-relaxed text-neutral-600">A flat artwork proof cannot tell you whether a printed corner will survive assembly.</p></div><img src={image} alt="Fictional Mereva cartons showing exposed white fibres along a dark printed folded corner beside a cleaner comparison corner" width="1536" height="1024" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" class="w-full rounded-xl" /></div></header>
<article class="container max-w-default prose-apex py-12 md:py-20">
<p>A printed carton can look right while it is flat and reveal a white fracture as soon as someone closes it. Approving the artwork alone leaves that failure undiscovered. For a Canadian or US buyer ordering folding cartons, the useful decision is whether the proposed board, finish and converting process can produce an acceptable assembled pack—not whether a supplier can send one attractive flat sheet.</p>
<p>Make folded appearance a purchase requirement before releasing the order. Specify which corners customers see, how the pack will be erected, and who can approve a visible deviation. This guide concerns solid-paperboard cartons; corrugated scoring and transit strength need their own evaluation.</p>
<h2>Describe the defect before requesting a remedy</h2>
<p>Keep an unfolded blank from the same sample batch beside the failed carton. Photograph the affected panel before folding, after normal erection and after closure. Mark the location on the dieline: a long side crease, a tuck flap or a small locking tab. Record whether every sample shows the problem or only particular positions. These observations give the converter a reproducible question instead of a vague request for better quality.</p>
<p>A pale line, lifted coating and an open split should not automatically receive the same diagnosis. Ask the converter to examine the affected layers and report what changed. Note whether the carton still closes, whether the corner opens under ordinary handling and whether loose material appears. A cosmetic acceptance decision does not establish functional suitability.</p>
<p>Do not mask the evidence with a touch-up marker or repeated hand-working of the crease. Retain the original failure, the matching blank and the assembly instructions. If the sample was folded backwards or forced beyond its intended travel, record that too; the comparison must represent the actual packing operation.</p>
<h2>Ask for the converting specification, not just heavier board</h2>
<p>Holmen Iggesund's <a href="https://www.iggesund.com/insights/paperboard-know-how/paperboard-manual/paperboard-manual-publication/printing-and-converting-performance/die-cutting-and-creasing/">paperboard converting guidance</a> explains that creasing creates a folding hinge and that tooling must account for board type, thickness and crease direction. It also identifies tool geometry, make-ready accuracy and die-cutter pressure as relevant factors. This is why a buyer should ask the converter to investigate the board-and-tool combination rather than prescribe a universal crease setting.</p>
<p>Request the exact board designation, nominal grammage, measured caliper, grain orientation on the dieline, surface finish and sample-making method. Ask which of those will be identical in production. The <a href="/blog/packaging-paper-gsm-thickness-guide">GSM and caliper guide</a> explains why a weight figure alone does not define the material. A substitution should trigger a review of the folded sample, even when the quoted GSM stays unchanged.</p>
<p>Have the converter propose the corrective trial and explain which variable it changes. That might mean a tooling adjustment, a revised board specification or a finish change. Leave numerical tooling settings to the converter and board supplier. A purchasing team can define the result it needs without pretending that one groove width or one grain-direction rule fits every carton.</p>
<h2>Separate a visual mock-up from a production trial</h2>
<p>Label every sample by what it can prove. A digitally printed mock-up may help assess artwork placement and size, but ask whether it uses the specified board, print process, coating and production creasing method. If any differ, record the limitation on the approval sheet. An attractive mock-up is not evidence that the intended production combination has passed.</p>
<p>For the approval trial, use the proposed finished construction and normal assembly sequence. Have the people who will pack the product erect and close samples with the actual contents or an agreed representative substitute. Inspect the customer-facing corners under consistent lighting at a written viewing distance. Use magnification for diagnosis separately from the agreed appearance check.</p>
<figure><img src="/images/blog/2026-11-28/folded-sample-inspection.webp" alt="Inspector folding a sage green fictional Sable Fern carton beside printed blanks and a magnifying loupe" width="1536" height="1024" loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Illustrative mock-brand scene: evaluate the folded pack as well as the flat blank.</figcaption></figure>
<p>Agree the handling and conditioning procedure with the converter, including storage before the test and the time between folding and inspection. Record the conditions rather than silently comparing samples prepared differently. For a closure meant to be reopened, specify the intended opening sequence and agree a repeat-use check. Do not impose repeated folding on a single-use corner and then describe that result as normal service failure.</p>
<h2>A comparison that isolates the proposed correction</h2>
<p>Consider an invented trial for a dark printed accessory carton. The buyer sees exposed white fibres on one customer-facing corner after erection. The converter offers a tooling revision and also suggests changing the surface finish. Testing both changes together would show whether the package improved, but would not identify which change was necessary. The following sequence keeps the purchasing decision traceable; it is not a claim about any material's performance.</p>
<div class="table-scroll" role="region" aria-label="Controlled carton crease trial" tabindex="0"><table><caption>Illustrative trial plan for a visible corner defect</caption><thead><tr><th>Sample group</th><th>What changes</th><th>What stays fixed</th><th>Decision it supports</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><td>A: reference</td><td>Nothing; reproduce the current sample</td><td>Board, artwork, finish and assembly</td><td>Can the reported defect be reproduced?</td></tr>
<tr><td>B: tooling trial</td><td>Converter's documented crease setup</td><td>Board, artwork, finish and assembly</td><td>Does the tooling proposal meet the agreed appearance and closure checks?</td></tr>
<tr><td>C: finish trial, if needed</td><td>Proposed finish, using the recorded setup from B</td><td>Board, artwork and assembly</td><td>Does the revised appearance justify a finish change and its quoted consequences?</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>
<p>Compare the complete results, including unsuccessful samples. If B meets the agreed checks, ask for its process record and a production-representative confirmation before paying for an additional finish change. If C is selected, reapprove colour, feel and closure alongside the crease. A correction is incomplete when it solves the corner but changes a feature the buyer never agreed to change.</p>
<p>Have quality and purchasing agree sample quantities, selection across the trial and lot acceptance rules before the work begins. This table is a diagnostic sequence, not a statistical sampling plan. Passing a handful of specially selected cartons cannot establish a defect rate for the full order.</p>
<h2>Write an appearance limit that receiving can apply</h2>
<p>Replace “no bad cracking” with a documented inspection method and reference samples. Identify the panels and crease locations covered, the assembled state, lighting, viewing distance and whether the pack contains its product. State how exposed fibres, coating lift, a split corner and closure interference are classified. The buyer and supplier should agree the limits; no universal visual tolerance is implied here.</p>
<p>Retain a signed acceptable sample and, where useful, an agreed boundary sample showing the largest permitted deviation. Label both with the drawing revision, board, finish and approval date. Give receiving the same reference that production receives. Photographs are useful for locating defects, but confirm that image lighting and scale do not conceal the feature being judged.</p>
<p>For <a href="/services/cardboard-boxes">custom folding-carton orders</a>, attach these controls to the specification rather than leaving them in an isolated email. Agree what happens to a shipment that fails: who holds stock, who investigates and who can authorize a concession. Do not let receiving quietly redefine an approved appearance limit because replacement stock is inconvenient.</p>
<h2>The release checklist belongs with the purchase order</h2>
<ul>
<li>The folded reference is identified by sample and dieline revision, with the inspected corners marked.</li>
<li>The supplier confirms the approved board, caliper, grain orientation, printing, finish and converting setup, plus which changes require renewed approval.</li>
<li>The packing owner confirms that the assembly and closure sequence matches actual use.</li>
<li>The buyer's quality owner signs the appearance limits, functional checks, sampling arrangements and receiving reference.</li>
<li>Purchasing accepts any revised charges and schedule after the correction, with no unconfirmed delivery commitment.</li>
<li>Production release is explicit; a flat artwork approval is not treated as approval of folded performance.</li>
</ul>
<p>A useful quote request includes photographs of the failure, an unfolded blank, the packed product dimensions and weight, the desired finish and the delivery destination. Ask for a proposed trial and the evidence required to release production, rather than simply requesting thicker stock.</p>
<p>From its base in London, Ontario, Apex Packaging Solutions coordinates packaging supply for Canadian and US businesses with qualified overseas production facilities. Materials, sampling, order quantities and delivery arrangements are confirmed for each project. <a href="/contact">Send Apex your carton brief and crease photographs</a> to discuss a sample review before placing the production order.</p></article><style>
